## Build output
/dist/
/bin/

## CodeQL
.cache/
.codeql/
# BQRS and SARIF files are blanket-ignored, because every run of the tool drops them wherever it
# was invoked. The negations below exempt `testdata/`, where the same extensions mean the opposite
# thing: a fixture the CLI generated once and the suite now depends on.
#
# Without them `git add` skips the fixture silently, and the test then passes on the machine that
# generated it and fails in CI as "no such file or directory" -- which reads as a broken test
# rather than as a file that was never committed. That cost a red build on PR #3.
*.bqrs
*.sarif
!**/testdata/**/*.bqrs
!**/testdata/**/*.sarif

## CodeQL query-test artifacts (.expected files are checked in; these are not)
*.testproj/
*.actual

## ABOM artifacts and databases
/analyses/
/output/
*.db
*.db.gz
*.zip
abom-graph.json
abom-dashboard.json

# A local declaration of the accounts this machine is responsible for. Untracked because ownership
# is one operator's statement, not a property of the repository — checking one in would declare
# somebody else's organization on every clone. It only ever fills a vacuum, and `gh` still decides
# whether what it declares is honored.
/abom.local.json

## Logs
/logs/

## Browser test harness. The lock file is checked in on purpose — it is the only record of what
## the one npm dependency actually resolved to.
ui/tests/e2e/node_modules/
ui/tests/e2e/test-results/
ui/tests/e2e/playwright-report/
ui/tests/e2e/blob-report/

# Agent working notes, deliberately untracked. Git history is already the record of what shipped;
# a markdown summary of it becomes a second copy that drifts from the log it duplicates. The
# reasoning behind each entry is worth keeping locally, so agents keep it here.
/plans/implemented.md

## .NET
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

## Everything this repository writes at runtime: the working corpus, logs, scratch, the published
## interface, the Go test framework's TMPDIR, and the prebuilt databases `make itest` reuses.
##
## One directory inside the checkout rather than the OS temp directory, which is world-writable,
## shared with every other process, and cleared on reboot on Linux -- where it would have taken
## the corpus with it. `make clean` deliberately does not remove it.
/.abom/

## OS generated, hidden files
.DS_Store

## Temporary by nature
*.swo
*.swp

